{{also|épilogue|épilogué}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|epilog}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fr|épilogue}}, from {{uder|en|la|epilogus}}, from {{uder|en|grc|ἐπίλογος||a conclusion, peroration of a speech, epilogue of a play}}, from {{m|grc|ἐπιλέγω||to say in addition}}. Eclipsed {{ncog|enm|lenvoie|t=epilogue}} borrowed ultimately from Old French.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛp.ɪ.lɒɡ/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-epilogue.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛpɪlɔɡ/|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛpɪlɑɡ/|a=cot-caught}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# A short [[speech]], [[spoken]] [[directly]] at the [[audience]] at the end of a [[play]]
# The [[performer]] who gives this speech
# A brief [[oration]] or [[script]] at the end of a [[literary]] [[piece]]; an [[afterword]]
# {{lb|en|computing}} A component of a [[computer program]] that prepares the computer to return from a [[routine]].

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|short speech at the end of a play}} {{l|en|endspeech}}
* {{sense|brief script at the end of a literary piece}} {{l|en|afterword}}, {{l|en|endspeech}}; see also [[Thesaurus:afterword]]

====Antonyms====
* {{antsense|short speech at the end of a play}} {{l|en|prologue}}
* {{antsense|brief script at the end of a literary piece}} {{l|en|prologue}}; see also [[Thesaurus:foreword]]

